                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "UPDATED REVIEW: I finished all Achievements after 8.6 hours. You MUST play through the game twice to get all Achievements since two Achievements are based on your final decision at the end of the game. I played through twice fully and two more times partially to get missing Achievements. I had to look up information for several of the Achievements because several are easy to miss or screw up. I still recommend this game experience. For the notoriously difficult rapid click interaction near the end, I ended up having to use my laptop built-in mouse because I could click faster than my external mouse. I would have liked the game experience more if I didn't have repeat the game multiple times to finish off the Achievements. I still think the story was good, even if something things don't make complete logical sense.\n\nThis is my first FMV game experience. I just finished my first play through at 2.9 Hours and 21/28 Achievements. That was with multiple restarts of certain sections due to the game design so an uninterrupted play through with no restarts would probably only take about 2.5 hours.\n\nI was very tempted to give this a thumbs down for one specific reason, but I did enjoy the game so I will give it a Recommend with a provision. At the very end of the game, there is a final player interaction that requires you to click the mouse or controller repeatedly. Up to that point, these types of interactions were easy with plenty of time. This last one was ridiculously short. After several failed attempts, the only solutions I could find were to download an auto-clicker (unacceptable) or to turn up the mouse sensitivity in the settings. This is absolute terrible game design and if adjusting the mouse sensitivity hadn’t worked this would definitely have been a thumbs down for me. But changing the sensitivity worked the first time I tried it.\n\nThis one interaction is really a shame since everything else was very good with the exception of one keypad puzzle (also near the end) that requires inputting 4 numbers with no real hints other than some dried blood on the keypad. I would never have solved that keypad puzzle either without looking it up because I missed one hard to see hint. But even seeing that hint, you would still have to try all the variations possible with 4 numbers and that would have taken a ridiculously long time. It was also immersion breaking since at that point in the game, the main character is supposed to be in a significant hurry. This puzzle really should have had some more specific hint.\nThose are my only two complaints. Other than that, the story was very good and interesting. The visuals, dialog, atmosphere, and acting were also very good, and the controls were simple even though you must figure out what to do with each interaction the first time you see it. The story had several very interesting plot twists and I enjoyed the story.\n\nI will go back and try to get the last 7 Achievements. I missed a few opportunities to click on things so we will see if another play through can finish off the missing Achievements. I will say that while the story was good, it probably is best not to think too deeply about the concept. If you approach the story as “psychological horror movie” as the Steam store page advertises, it seems enjoyable. In fact, the story concept could have made a decent movie, at least in my opinion.\n\nSo, other than the one interaction at the very end and the keypad puzzle, I really enjoyed the overall game and the story. I certainly don’t regret my experience with the game, but I would have been rather unhappy if I couldn’t get passed that end game interaction. I wouldn't pay full price for this game. I bought it when it was on a significant sale and I recommend doing that if you are interested in experiencing the story.\n",
